mighty number nine might not bear the  megaman name but there's no escaping the  fact that it's a spiritual successor of  the classic side-scrolling series you  run jump and shoot it any robots through  a series of 2d levels and for every boss  you defeat you gained a new ability to  use during the rest of your journey  that's not to mention longtime Megaman  producer Keiji Inafune a is at the helm  there's more to money number nine than  elements of just Megaman however you're  encouraged to be fast and efficient you  could argue this is just a reflection of  the Megaman X Games where skillful  dashing was required in order to  complete certain levels or reach secret  areas but money number nine rewards  quick and nimble play in a new way  by allowing you to absorb particles from  damaged enemies that temporarily enhance  your speed armor and power these boosts  have an immediate and tangible effect  and the more you play the more skilled  you become with both acquiring and using  them money number nine sense of speed  evokes intermittent exhilaration and his  systolic elements can you charm it but  these spurts of joy aren't enough to  carry a game in the face of its standout  issues  there are a total of 12 stages to  complete in story mode before that you  unlock after you finish the initial 8  these 8 levels can be tackled in any  order you wish but don't mistake this  open-ended level selection as a true  call back to Mega Man's punishing  structure where you once had to decipher  the correct path through stages and  ultimately bosses money number nines  bosses can all be defeated using your  default weapon with reasonable effort  unless you rarely feel compelled to  explore your weapon upgrades the last 4  stages are an exception where obstacles  in particular demand the use of one  weapon or another but at that point you  have no attachment to your newfound  tools and thus only a vague sense of  pride in your progress  the real challenge lies in earning high  ranks killing groups of enemies in a  specific way that earns e accolades is  an ever-present challenge marks are  determined by factors like how much  damage you take how many enemies you  kill and how efficient you are tubes  orbing their energy perfecting your  reflexes is a fun pursuit if only  because of the rewarding feelings you  get from chaining together attacks and  dashes in a fast and stylish manner but  it's nothing more than a convenient way  to spice up money number 9s typical  action  at best the games 3d models look like  sharp and expressive recreations of  classic game art but mighty number 9 s  presentation mostly feels like a  compromised effort failing to deliver  either contemporary Flair or classic  whimsy cutscenes are particularly  disappointing but characters that simply  sway in place as they talk through  static mouths  you can choose to hear dialog in either  Japanese or English but expect to switch  to Japanese regardless of your habitual  preference the English voice acting is  too slow and abrasive to stomach the  Japanese government won't let us  intervene until we know the cause of the  malfunctions hmm I was afraid of that  but it makes sense after all any robots  you send to help could be affected as  well making things even worse for a game  that's meant to bear the legacy of a  classic series mighty number 9 barely  succeeds it may rouse excitement from  time to time but by and large it lacks a  pervading sense of artistry both in its  level design and presentation mighty  number 9 is an inoffensive ly average  game sprung from the memories of the  past with little to show for its  position in the present  for
 

